3. Thermal Management — Finite‑Element & CFD Analysis

3.1 FEA Thermal Model

Detailed 3D FEA model (SolidWorks/Ansys) of temple assembly, including graphite heat spreader (150 µm, in‑plane $k = 1500\ \mathrm{W/m\cdot K}$), battery, and PCB. Boundary conditions:

· Natural convection: $h = 8\ \mathrm{W/m^2K}$ (still air) on outer surfaces.
· Skin contact: isothermal 35 °C on inner temple region.
· Hot‑spot power: SoC die area 5 mm², 600 mW.

FEA Results (simulated):
With spreader, junction‑to‑skin $\Delta T = 14.3\ \mathrm{°C}$ at 600 mW, well within 18 °C budget. Without spreader: 24 °C → confirmed necessity.

3.2 CFD Validation

Airflow around temple in walking conditions (1 m/s relative wind). Forced convection $h \approx 15\ \mathrm{W/m^2K}$.
Net $R_{\text{th}}$ decreases to ~22 K/W → additional thermal margin.
Hot‑climate (30 °C ambient, no wind): $\Delta T$ budget reduces to 13 °C; system must limit sustained power to 520 mW (with spreader). This bound is enforced by firmware.

