10. Cybersecurity & Privacy‑by‑Design Architecture

10.1 Threat Model (STRIDE)

Threat Mitigation
Unauthorized firmware update Secure boot chain: HW root of trust (PUF key) → first‑stage bootloader (signed) → TEE‑verified OS
Eavesdropping on audio All audio encrypted (TLS 1.3) before leaving device; local ASR no cloud
Camera abuse Hardware LED tied to power rail; 30 s auto‑stop; physical shutter detection
Cloud provider compromise On‑device anomaly detection; no raw sensor data to cloud; only anonymized transcripts

10.2 Secure Boot & Key Management

· PUF‑derived unique device key (UDK), stored in on‑die SRAM PUF.
· Boot stages verified using ECDSA P‑384 signatures.
· All OTA updates encrypted and signed, rollback protection via anti‑fuse fuses.
· User biometric (voiceprint) stored in TEE, never leaves device.

10.3 Privacy Architecture

· Data minimization: raw audio/video never leaves device without explicit user action (photo/video sharing).
· All AI training data derived from synthetic logs, not user content.
· On‑device federated learning for wake‑word adaptation, with differential privacy guarantees ($\epsilon = 2.0, \delta = 10^{-6}$).

